"""lxdtop: a live, btop-style view of an LXD host, one row per container or VM.

Run it on the LXD host itself, in a terminal or over SSH. It is laid out for a
160x45 screen (a small console monitor or a large terminal window) and adapts
to smaller windows. On a shorter one the CPU graph shrinks, the stopped
instances share a line and only the top running ones are listed before a panel
is dropped; a narrower one drops panels, then columns. It uses only the Python
standard library: copy this one file to the host (for example ~/bin/lxdtop)
and run it.

    lxdtop            run it (? lists the keys: q quits, s sorts, / filters, Enter shows details)
    lxdtop --dump     print one frame as plain text and exit (for testing)
    lxdtop --drawing classic    draw as on the Linux console, in a terminal window too
    lxdtop --dump --json    print one reading as a JSON document, for scripts

Top to bottom: a status line (LXD version, clock, counts); host panels for
CPU, memory and network; CPU use stacked by instance beside a live feed of
LXD events; then one row per container or VM.

Everything it does is read-only. Each refresh it reads:
    /sys/fs/cgroup/lxc.payload.<name>/      CPU, memory and task counters per container, and its
                                            limits, stalls (*.pressure) and memory kills
    /proc/<pid>/stat, /proc/<pid>/cgroup    the busiest process inside each container,
                                            and each VM's QEMU process (CPU, memory)
    /proc/spl/kstat/zfs/<pool>/objset-*     disk reads and writes per instance (ZFS)
    /proc/spl/kstat/zfs/<pool>/state        each ZFS pool's health (ONLINE, DEGRADED, ...)
    /sys/class/net/<nic>/statistics/        network bytes per instance (veth/tap) and host port
    /proc/stat, /proc/meminfo, hwmon, ...   host totals, temperature, clock speed
It also talks to LXD over its local socket, GET requests and an event stream
only: every 15 seconds for names, state, addresses and pool usage, and
continuously for lifecycle events. That socket needs membership in the lxd
group (or root). Without it lxdtop still runs, minus IPs, the network
columns, VMs, stopped instances and the event feed.

Needs Linux with cgroup v2 (Ubuntu 22.04 or later), LXD 5 or 6, and Python 3.
Tested on Ubuntu 24.04 with LXD 6.7 and Python 3.12.
"""

